54:   );
55:   return data.createLimitedAccessToken.limitedAccessToken.tokenHeader;
56: }"}},"label":"answer-1","response":{"answer":"No. The originally proposed primary path—CLI packages a tarball and the Worker builds it remotely via Fly's Machines API—was not implemented. The builder found it unworkable: Fly has no build-from-tarball REST endpoint, and remote builds require speaking the Docker/BuildKit wire protocol to a builder machine, which a Cloudflare Worker cannot do (d1, m2077; see the code comment in packages/control-plane/src/fly.js lines 1-6 documenting this rationale directly).

What shipped instead: the Worker holds the org-scoped FLY_API_TOKEN privately and mints a per-app, 1-hour-lived deploy token (via Fly's createLimitedAccessToken GraphQL mutation) that it hands to the CLI (d1, d2; fly.js lines 48-56). The CLI then runs `flyctl deploy` locally using that scoped token (packages/cli/lib/fly.js lines 37-44), rather than the Worker performing the build itself. This also fixed a security gap where any authenticated user could get org-wide machine credentials (d2, m1812).

