**What shipped instead:** a token-brokering split. The Worker (control-plane) holds the long-lived org admin `FLY_API_TOKEN` and never exposes it to the CLI. It only creates/ensures the Fly app and shared IPv4 (`ensureFlyApp`, fly.js:22-34) and mints a short-lived, app-scoped deploy token via GraphQL (`mintDeployToken`, expiring in 1h, fly.js:48-56). The actual build/deploy is delegated to `flyctl` running wherever the CLI executes — it invokes `flyctl deploy . --remote-only ...` with that minted token (packages/cli/lib/fly.js:37-44), letting Fly's own remote builder machines speak Docker/BuildKit, which the Worker environment can't do.

So the build step is Fly-server-side ("remote-only"), but it's triggered by the CLI process using a scoped token, not directly initiated from inside the Worker via a tarball upload.

**Next step:** if a true Worker-driven build is still wanted, check "docs/v3" referenced in the comment for whatever alternative (e.g., different builder API) was being tracked there — it's not present in this snapshot."}},{"available_context":{"messages":[{"id":"m1812","original_text_sha256":"be855bea7a06f943f66b378725509a882c7bd3fe92492cf401be0f548b4dba1b","redacted":false,"role":"user","source_line":1812,"source_message_id":"6460883e-50b9-4ceb-af43-31fafca538d7","text":"Move the Fly deploy out of the CLI and into the control plane. Today /api/deploy returns the org-wide Fly token and the CLI runs flyctl with it — any authenticated user gets credentials for every machine in the org. That must end before a second user exists.

New flow: the CLI packages the app directory into a tarball and POSTs it to /api/deploy with small.toml and secrets. The Worker holds FLY_API_TOKEN as a secret and does everything itself via the Fly Machines API: create the Fly app if missing, trigger a remote build from the tarball, create or update the machine with the image, secrets, and SMALL_PROXY_SECRET, allocate a shared IPv4 on first deploy, and store the origin in D1. The CLI never sees a Fly token, never shells out to flyctl, and never contains anything Fly-specific. Remove the flyctl dependency from packages/cli entirely.

If a remote build from a tarball via the Machines API is not workable, fall back to: the Worker mints a scoped Fly deploy token limited to that one app, expiring in one hour, and returns that to the CLI instead of the org token. Document which path you took and why.

Keep the integration test passing: deploy examples/counter end to end, confirm the fly.dev origin still 403s, and add an assertion that the CLI's config file and network traffic contain no Fly token. then update v1 and v2 if needed","timestamp":"2026-09-04T02:13:04.199Z"},{"id":"m1824","original_text_sha256":"228bdb2e9aa00d24b95ce881f0be5d137a6b1b5aff4ad473d6b85ef8dad4fc28","redacted":false,"role":"assistant","source_line":1824,"source_message_id":"42a81c2a-85f4-4ad3-a4e1-f24633ce691c","text":"Big one. Plan:

1. Spike: verify Fly GraphQL `createLimitedAccessToken` (scoped 1h deploy token) + Machines API app create actually work from plain HTTPS — decides primary vs fallback
2. Worker: create Fly app + allocate IP + mint scoped token itself; stop returning org token
3. CLI 0.0.4: drop `apps create` (worker does it), publish
4. Tests: counter e2e + 403 + no-token assertions + scoped-token-can't-touch-other-apps
5. Docs v1/v2 updates, commit, push

Up front on the primary path: Fly has no REST "build from tarball" endpoint — remote builds speak the Docker/BuildKit wire protocol to a builder machine, which a Worker can't (no raw TCP, no docker client). So the scoped-token fallback is the workable route; spike will confirm. Verifying now:","timestamp":"2026-09-04T02:14:35.543Z"},{"id":"m1940","original_text_sha256":"5d1df26113527292e43cae51809279e415f287eafdd29582a6a86a5b48a4ac4f","redacted":false,"role":"assistant","source_line":1940,"source_message_id":"fc25caff-e7e3-43c3-8fbf-2598375a94ab","text":"All code is ready and green (CLI 10/10, worker rewired, tests written). **Blocked on you now** — everything left needs the org admin token:

1. Go to https://fly.io/dashboard/personal/tokens
2. Create token → type **Admin** (org-wide, not app-scoped) — this is what lets the worker mint the 1h per-app tokens
3. Replace the `FLY_API_TOKEN=...` line in `.env` with it (current one is stale anyway — it can't even manage machines)

Then say "done" and I'll: verify mint works with it → set worker secret + deploy worker → publish CLI 0.0.4 → run the full integration suite (counter e2e, fly.dev 403, no-token-leak, scoped-token-401-on-other-app) → update docs v1/v2 → commit and push.

What's staged, for the record:
- Worker `/api/deploy` and `/api/logs` no longer return the org token — worker creates the Fly app, allocates the shared IPv4, and mints a **1h deploy token scoped to that one app** via Fly's GraphQL `createLimitedAccessToken`. Fails closed (502) if minting fails.
- CLI 0.0.4: `flyctl apps create` removed (worker owns it). flyctl remains for build/deploy per the sanctioned fallback — Fly has no tarball-build REST API; remote builds need the Docker/BuildKit protocol a Worker can't speak. Will document exactly this.
- New tests: config + stdout must contain no `FlyV1`/`fm2_`; the returned token must list its own app's machines (200) and get rejected (401/403) on another app's.","timestamp":"2026-09-04T02:19:16.357Z"},{"id":"m1943","original_text_sha256":"6c684cb20e3bcec17d84941ca6116b0bf86c48ecf49f952e279b7aabb4303943","redacted":false,"role":"user","source_line":1943,"source_message_id":"c5a3a871-33e8-45d2-8c99-090b8dda4fe9","text":"there is only org token that i can create","timestamp":"2026-09-04T02:22:39.434Z"},{"id":"m1953","original_text_sha256":"9f19ca361cca965bec9e17e54b51c9be17659dcf301296004b46ebddbb725a32","redacted":false,"role":"assistant","source_line":1953,"source_message_id":"c72b7258-83e0-451e-a3b8-afab541b08f5","text":"Org token might work — create it, paste into `.env` as `FLY_API_TOKEN=`, tell me done, and I'll test whether it can mint scoped tokens.

